Title: /roar saves memory at step 6 but cannot write it until step 9
Created: 2026-09-08
Author: sim

--- log ---
2026-09-08T08:04Z sim created
2026-09-08T08:04Z sim note observed live at the end of a long session in search-works-for-zotero. Step 6 was reached inside the worktree, every write to `projects/*/memory/**` was refused, and the lessons only landed because step 9 came a few minutes later and I retried afterwards. A session that ended at step 6, or an agent that read the refusal as "memory is not available here", would have lost them silently

--- body ---

## Context

`skills/roar/SKILL.md` orders its steps: **6. Save persistent memory**, then
**9. Exit worktree**. During a worktree session, step 6 cannot write.

`rules/workflow.md` § Worktree paths already records why, and records it
correctly:

> Memory files (`projects/*/memory/**`) are documented as a carve-out — [the
> hook] exempts that path explicitly — but that custom hook is not the only gate
> in front of a primary-checkout write. Once a session has called
> `EnterWorktree`, a separate platform-native Edit/Write guard tied to the
> session's tracked worktree path also fires, has no memory exemption, and
> denies the write. **Treat the carve-out as inert during an active worktree
> session.**

So the rule knows. What nothing states is the consequence for `/roar`, whose
own step order walks straight into it: the step that saves the lessons runs
three steps before the step that makes saving possible.

## Why this loses work rather than merely annoying

The failure is silent in the direction that matters. A denied write returns a
refusal an agent can easily read as "memory is unavailable in this context",
and the natural response is to summarise the lesson in the final message
instead — where it is read once and never again. Nothing downstream notices: the
worktree is removed at step 9, the session ends, and the absence of a memory
file is indistinguishable from a session that had no lesson worth saving.

Observed 2026-09-08 (search-works-for-zotero, a session that merged five PRs and
filed five upstream issues, two pull requests and a private security report).
Two durable lessons and one reference entry were written **only** because step 9
happened to follow within a few minutes and the write was retried from the
primary checkout afterwards. Neither the skill nor the rule told me to do that;
the retry was luck.

## Options considered

1. **Reorder the skill** so memory is saved after the worktree exit. Cheapest,
   and correct for the common case. It does move a "reflect" step away from the
   reflection block, which is a small loss of readability.
2. **Keep step 6 where it is and add one sentence** naming the constraint and
   telling the agent to defer the write until after step 9. Preserves the
   narrative order; costs a line.
3. **Make the platform guard honour the carve-out.** The right fix in principle
   and not ours to make — the deny comes from a platform-native gate, not from
   `scripts/pretooluse-worktree-path-guard.sh`, which already exempts the path.

Option 3 is out of reach, so the choice is between 1 and 2. Recommend **2**:
the ordering constraint is a fact about the environment rather than about the
wrap-up, and a skill that explains *why* a step is deferred survives a future
runtime that lifts the restriction, where a silently reordered list would just
look arbitrary.

## Actions

1. Amend `skills/roar/SKILL.md` step 6 to state the constraint and to require
   the write after step 9 when the session is in a worktree.
2. Amend `rules/workflow.md` § Worktree paths with the one-line corollary: a
   wrap-up that saves memory must do so after leaving the worktree.
3. Check the sibling wrap-up skills — `/lair` runs a memory sweep, `/dream`
   consolidates memory — for the same ordering hazard, and fix whichever has it.

## Test

`tests/test_roar_step2_attribution.py` extracts and runs a fenced block from
step 2; whatever is added here must not introduce a second fenced block in that
step. A text assertion that step 6 names the constraint is the cheap guard, on
the model of the existing skill-text tests.

## Verification

- [x] Step 6 names the constraint and the deferral
- [x] `rules/workflow.md` carries the corollary
- [x] `/lair` and `/dream` checked for the same hazard: `/dream` is immune by an
      earlier decision (0247 — it deliberately runs outside a worktree, since a
      worktree run would commit onto the primary checkout's branch anyway), and
      `/lair` reaches memory only by delegating to it at step 11. So the defect
      is `/roar`-specific, though a worktree-isolated session invoking that
      chain meets the same platform gate; noted in the rule rather than
      duplicated into two more skills

## Invariants

- Step 2's single-fenced-block invariant holds
- No change to the worktree guards themselves; this is a documentation and
  ordering fix

## Exit criteria

- A session running `/roar` from inside a worktree is told, before it tries,
  that the memory write must wait for step 9
